  "state": {
    "case_id": "34",
    "case_summary": "A 32-year-old woman, in her first pregnancy, was regularly seen at our antenatal outpatient clinic. Her medical history included cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN II) treated by loop excision two years prior and a bilateral breast reduction with complicated but spontaneous wound healing 13 years earlier. At 18 weeks of gestation, she was noted to have chronic hypertension with a maximum diastolic blood pressure of 95 mm Hg. At 31 weeks and 4 days, she presented with lower abdominal pain and fever. Two weeks earlier, she had experienced flu-like symptoms, headache, and a sore throat. Physical examination revealed a body temperature of 38.3°C, uterine contractions, and a fundal height corresponding to 29 weeks of gestation. The cervix was 1 cm dilated and completely effaced, with intact amniotic membranes. Ultrasound showed fetal growth at the fifth percentile and oligohydramnios. Fetal cardiotocogram (CTG) indicated a variable fetal heart rate of 160 beats per minute with late decelerations. External tocodynamometry recorded uterine contractions. Blood tests showed a high white blood cell count (WBC) of 24.4 × 10^9/L with neutrophilia and an elevated C-reactive protein (CRP) of 170 mg/L. Intravenous amoxicillin clavulanate and ritodrine were administered after a vaginal culture was taken. The contractions decreased, but fetal heart rate decelerations persisted. A caesarean section was performed, resulting in the birth of a healthy boy weighing 1227 grams (5th–10th percentile) with 1 and 5-minute Apgar scores of 9 and 10, respectively. Postoperatively, the woman continued on amoxicillin clavulanate, her temperature normalized, and CRP dropped to 84 mg/L. On day 5 postpartum, she developed a spiking fever. Physical examination of the lungs, breasts, and wound did not indicate infection. CRP rose to 321 mg/L and WBC was 27.5 × 10^9/L. Cultures from the vagina, throat, urine, and stool were negative. Serologic tests for toxoplasmosis, syphilis, rubella, cytomegalovirus, and herpes simplex virus were negative. By day 8 postpartum, she still had a spiking fever and elevated CRP despite amoxicillin clavulanate. Abdominal ultrasound and chest X-ray were normal. Amoxicillin clavulanate was discontinued. Nine days after delivery, the Pfannenstiel wound developed purulent discharge and small peri-incisional erythema; the wound borders were closed. On day 11 postpartum, a wound culture indicated staphylococcus aureus, and flucloxacillin was started. The definitive culture was negative, and flucloxacillin was switched to oral clindamycin on day 17 postpartum. The wound became more erythematous, with blue-red colored margins, infiltration, and gaping at the corners. CRP was 85 mg/L and WBC 17.7 × 10^9/L. Standard conservative wound care was recommended, and the woman was discharged to home care 19 days after delivery. Twenty-three days after the caesarean section, she was readmitted due to severe wound pain. CRP was 409 mg/L and WBC 34.1 × 10^9/L. The wound borders had a violaceous color and the ulcerative defect was progressively expanding.\n\nHistopathologic findings of the wound biopsy showed necrosis of the epidermis with edema and bulla and an intense infiltration of granulocytes in the dermis. There were no signs of vasculitis and immunofluorescence assay was negative for immune complex vasculitis.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
